<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Didessa River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Didessa is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and a major tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a>, the Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. In the 19th century its upper valley was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> kingdoms of Gomma, Gumma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmu">Limmu</a>-Ennarea, whose wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, gold and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/didessa-river.jpg" alt="Didessa River" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Braxmeier &amp;amp; Peter in s, Location map:Sting · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbay_OSM.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
